# Hosted {{.Topology.Features.Deployment}} deployment

Review-only, Bicep-validated infrastructure. No resources or services have been
deployed by generation. [MANIFEST.md](MANIFEST.md) shows architecture and TOSCA
dependencies; [RULES.md](RULES.md) records security/reliability tradeoffs.
[AGENT-MCP.md](AGENT-MCP.md) documents optional Actions agent access through
both GITHUB_TOKEN and signed OIDC provenance. Enablement is separate from
browser OAuth and is never inferred from this plan.

{{if eq .Topology.Features.Deployment "single-vm"}}
Exactly one VM hosts the Go dashboard and local PostgreSQL. PostgreSQL 16 is
installed by cloud-init, but CAO databases, roles and runtime are NOT activated.
Use Unix sockets/loopback only; never publish port 5432. The managed data disk
must be mounted and PostgreSQL's data directory moved to it before use.
No managed PostgreSQL, Functions, ACR, target Key Vault, Container Apps or ADX
service is created.
{{else}}
Exactly one VM hosts the Go dashboard; Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server holds
canonical data and isolated operational tables. The database is private through
a delegated subnet and linked private DNS. No Functions, ACR, target Key Vault,
Container Apps or ADX service is created. This minimizes managed services,
not VM administration or downtime risk.
{{end}}

An EXISTING Key Vault remains mandatory for credentials, TLS key material and
the restricted runtime URL. runtime-secret-references.json contains references,
never values. Infrastructure managed identity is provisioned, but grants are
NOT widened automatically. Approve secret-scoped access and secure delivery
separately. Do not copy secrets into Bicep, cloud-init, policy or this folder.

## Ordered activation

1. Replace example identities, public SSH key and documentation-only CIDRs.
   Review name availability, subscription/region, SKUs and the fixed
   10.44.0.0/16 network for overlap. Default SKUs are development choices.
2. Read TODO.md and GITHUB-APPS.md. Run `bash scripts/validate.sh` and
   `bash scripts/what-if.sh SUBSCRIPTION_ID RESOURCE_GROUP`.
   Deployment requires a separate approval/process; scripts never apply.
3. Use approved private administration/Run Command: SSH is NOT public.
   Verify cloud-init, positively identify the new empty disk, then mount it.
   No generated script formats disks or destroys data.
4. Follow the generating revision's server/POSTGRES.md. Create distinct
   owner/runtime/retention/evidence roles and a dedicated schema. Owner-run
   bootstrap/partition maintenance must succeed before runtime.
   The web process must NEVER receive the administrator/owner URL.
5. Ingest a verified Actions snapshot or explicitly selected empty evidence;
   never treat a missing snapshot as empty. Schedule subsequent ingestion.
6. Supply an approved standalone Linux Go server binary with embedded
   dashboard/query resources, reviewed host policy and private runtime
   environment. Use the existing hosted/container profile with PostgreSQL
   operational storage and one replica; this is not SQLite or volatile memory.
7. Fetch secrets/certificates from Key Vault through approved scoped access.
   Configure CAO_POSTGRES_URL with the runtime login (verified TLS for managed
   PostgreSQL), CAO_SESSION_SECRET, CAO_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID/CLIENT_SECRET,
   CAO_GITHUB_ALLOWED_ORGS/TEAMS/USERS, CAO_GITHUB_ADMIN_USERS and
   CAO_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET. Preserve both run/linked retention settings.
8. Serve DIRECT TLS using `cao-dashboard serve-hosted --listen 0.0.0.0:443
   --cert /etc/cao/tls/server.crt --key /etc/cao/tls/server.key
   --operational-store postgres`. A dedicated non-root service account may
   receive only CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE to bind 443. Protect policy, environment
   and certificate files; avoid a privileged container or root web process.
9. Register the actual dashboardHostName output as the allowed host and set
   its exact HTTPS OAuth callback. Verify health/readiness, rejected signed-out
   requests, authorized/unauthorized sign-in and database transport.

The VM's explicit public IP provides egress; approved source CIDRs restrict
HTTPS ingress. NSGs deny all other inbound ports, including SSH and PostgreSQL.
This is not a WAF, an outbound allowlist, HA or production acceptance.
Key Vault existence/access, TLS, backups and live runtime effectiveness remain
independently unverified. Turning profiles off is not teardown.
